{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>The first of your grain.</b> Rashi's explanation: one fiftieth, an average amount. And the verse calls it אחד אחוז, one held, from the fifty. The first terumah is called terumah gedolah, the great terumah, because its measure is greater than the measures of first tithe and of terumat ma'aser. How so? After it has been separated — being one part of the fifty — one must deduct the forty-nine remaining parts and divide them into fifty equal parts, in order to take from them five parts for first tithe." "<b>And the first of the fleece of your sheep.</b> It applies only in the Land, according to Rabbi Elai in the chapter Roshit HaGez." "<b>Your sheep.</b> Excludes a treifah." "<b>You shall give him.</b> That it must be of a sufficient amount for a gift. From here they said: in Judea one gives the weight of five selas, which are ten in the Galilee."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The phrase אחד אחוז מן החמישים is a wordplay on Numbers 18:21; the arithmetic describes how terumat ma'aser is calculated from the remainder after the great terumah.", 1 "Roshit HaGez is the name of a Talmudic chapter (Chullin 11:4 in the Mishnah, corresponding to Chullin 135a–137a in the Gemara).", 2 "טריפה refers to an animal with a fatal defect that renders it forbidden under Jewish law.", 3 "The phrase משקל ה׳ סלעים likely means 'a weight of five selas'; 'ten in the Galilee' refers to a different regional weight standard (shekels or denarii)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:53:22.004070324Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}